  "@type": "SoftwareSourceCode",
  "identifier": "crywolf",
  "description": "Fits occupancy models that correct for false positives from automated detectors, such as acoustic classifiers, using the multiple-methods (site-confirmation) design of Miller et al. (2011) <doi:10.1890/10-1396.1>, in which a subset of detections is verified, or the observation-confirmation design of Chambert et al. (2015) <doi:10.1890/14-1507.1>. Three models are available: single-season, dynamic (multi-season, with colonization and extinction), and multispecies, with species-level effects drawn from community distributions. Models are written in 'Stan' and fitted through 'cmdstanr'. The false-positive rate can be constant or depend on site covariates, and the true-detection process accepts observation covariates and a recorder random effect. The package also simulates data under homogeneous, unstructured, and structured false-positive regimes and runs simulation-based calibration checks, so users can ask whether the correction keeps nominal interval coverage for their own design and how much verification it needs.",
  "name": "crywolf: Bayesian False-Positive Occupancy Models with Calibration Checks",
